<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oran</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wahran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oran is a port city on the north-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, founded in 902 by Andalusi sailors. It was a trading port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayyanid-dynasty">Zayyanids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, Spanish from 1509 to 1708 and again from 1732 to 1792, and then the seat of the Ottoman bey of the west. Under French rule from 1831 it became Algeria's second city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oran.jpg" alt="Oran" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: R hakka · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:31-4_Chapelle_de_Santa-Cruz_(7).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oran is a coastal city in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, 432 kilometres west-south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. The name Wahran is thought to come from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> expression meaning place of lions, and the hypothesis linking it to the Libyco-Berber root for lion is regarded as the most plausible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Phoenician settlement in the region dates to the Punic period, between the 6th and 1st centuries BCE, and a small Roman settlement, Unica Colonia, existed near the site until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, the Mauro-Roman kingdom and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in turn took the region. The present city was founded around the start of the 10th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> of the Maghrawa confederation. It prospered under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a>, who built thirty vessels to connect it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, and under the Zyyanid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, which used its port as an outlet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-navarro">Pedro Navarro</a> captured the city for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 17 May 1509, on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jimenez-de-cisneros">Cardinal Cisneros</a>. Spanish rule lasted until 1708, when Mustapha Ben Youssef, bey of Mascara and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the dey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, took advantage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> to drive the Spanish out. Spanish forces returned in 1732. On the night after 8 October 1790 an earthquake killed more than 3,000 people in less than seven minutes, and Charles IV, finding the occupation costly and perilous, agreed with the dey of Algiers to leave. The bey Mohammed el Kebir besieged the city, and the Spanish evacuated by February 1792. Oran then became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>, which lasted until the French captured it in 1831.</p><p>Under French rule Oran became the second largest Algerian city, and many Europeans settled there, forming a majority of its population by the early 20th century. In July 1940 the British navy shelled French warships in the port, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> took the city in November 1942 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-torch">Operation Torch</a>. During the Algerian War of 1954 to 1962 Oran had one of the highest shares of Europeans of any North African city. In July 1962 the Oran massacre killed at least 95 and as many as 365 people, and most Europeans and Algerian Jews left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Oran is the capital of Oran Province and, with a population of 803,329 in 2008, the second city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. Under French rule it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Département</a> of Oran. The Pasha Mosque, built in 1796 by the bey with ransom money paid for the release of Spanish prisoners, dates from the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tashfin's death near <b>Oran</b> in 1145 opened Morocco to the Almohads.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Franco seriously considered joining for the sake of ideology and of claims to Gibraltar, French Morocco and the <b>Oran</b> district of Algeria, and took over the international city of Tangier after the defeat of France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1942 Allied forces landed at Algiers and <b>Oran</b> in Operation Torch, and in 1943 Algiers became the seat of the French Committee of National Liberation, headed jointly by Charles de Gaulle and Henri Giraud until de Gaulle took sole control; Algerian units then fought in Italy…</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of Granada, Spain seized a chain of North African ports, among them Mers el-Kébir in 1505, <b>Oran</b> in 1509 and Bejaia in 1510, and around 1510 it built a fort on the Peñón, an islet commanding the harbour of Algiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mustapha Bouchelaghem captured <b>Oran</b> in 1708, though Spain retook it in 1732.</blockquote><a href="/state/beylik-of-the-west">Beylik of the West</a></li><li><blockquote>Its exact borders are unknown, but it was centred on the Tlemcen region and the <b>Oran</b> plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-moors-and-romans">Kingdom of the Moors and Romans</a></li></ul></section>
